BGPCTL(8)                 BSD System Manager's Manual                BGPCTL(8)

NNAAMMEE
     bbggppccttll — control the BGP routing daemon

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     bbggppccttll [--jjnnVV] [--ss _s_o_c_k_e_t] _c_o_m_m_a_n_d [_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The bbggppccttll program controls the bgpd(8) daemon.  Commands may be abbrevi‐
     ated to the minimum unambiguous prefix; for example, ss ssuu for sshhooww
     ssuummmmaarryy.

     The options are as follows:

     --jj      Create output as JSON object.

     --nn      Show neighbors' IP addresses instead of their description.

     --ss _s_o_c_k_e_t
             Use _s_o_c_k_e_t to communicate with bgpd(8) instead of the default
             _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/_b_g_p_d_._s_o_c_k_._<_r_d_o_m_a_i_n_> where _<_r_d_o_m_a_i_n_> is the routing do‐
             main bbggppccttll is running in.  To administer bgpd(8) in a different
             routing domain, run bbggppccttll in said routing domain.

     --VV      Show the version and exit.

     The commands are as follows:

     ffiibb [ttaabbllee _n_u_m_b_e_r] ccoouuppllee
             Insert the learned routes into the specified Forwarding Informa‐
             tion Base a.k.a. the kernel routing table.

     ffiibb [ttaabbllee _n_u_m_b_e_r] ddeeccoouuppllee
             Remove the learned routes from the specified Forwarding Informa‐
             tion Base a.k.a. the kernel routing table.

     fflloowwssppeecc aadddd _f_a_m_i_l_y _r_u_l_e [sseett _a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t _._._.]
             Add the specified flowspec rule to the list of announced rules.
             Currently _f_a_m_i_l_y can be either iinneett or iinneett66.  It is possible to
             set various path attributes with additional arguments.  Adding a
             rule will replace an existing equal rule, including rules loaded
             from the configuration.  See bgpd.conf(5) for information on how
             to write a flowspec rule.

     fflloowwssppeecc ddeelleettee _f_a_m_i_l_y _r_u_l_e
             Remove the specified flowspec rule from the list of announced
             rules.

     fflloowwssppeecc fflluusshh
             Remove all dynamically added (i.e. with bbggppccttll fflloowwssppeecc aadddd)
             flowspec rules from the list of announced rules.

     fflloowwssppeecc sshhooww _f_a_m_i_l_y
             Show all announced flowspec rules.  _f_a_m_i_l_y, if given, limits the
             output to the given address family.  The supported families are
             _i_n_e_t and _i_n_e_t_6.

     lloogg bbrriieeff
             Disable verbose debug logging.

     lloogg vveerrbboossee
             Enable verbose debug logging.

     nneeiigghhbboorr _p_e_e_r cclleeaarr [_r_e_a_s_o_n]
             Stop and restart the BGP session to the specified neighbor.  If a
             _r_e_a_s_o_n is provided, the _r_e_a_s_o_n is sent as Administrative Shutdown
             Communication to the neighbor.  _p_e_e_r may be the neighbor's ad‐
             dress, description or the word ggrroouupp followed by a group descrip‐
             tion.

     nneeiigghhbboorr _p_e_e_r ddeessttrrooyy
             Destroy a previously cloned peer.  The peer must be down before
             calling this function.  _p_e_e_r may be the neighbor's address, de‐
             scription or the word ggrroouupp followed by a group description.

     nneeiigghhbboorr _p_e_e_r ddoowwnn [_r_e_a_s_o_n]
             Take the BGP session to the specified neighbor down.  If a _r_e_a_s_o_n
             is provided, the _r_e_a_s_o_n is sent as Administrative Shutdown Commu‐
             nication to the neighbor.  _p_e_e_r may be the neighbor's address,
             description or the word ggrroouupp followed by a group description.

     nneeiigghhbboorr _p_e_e_r rreeffrreesshh
             Request the neighbor to re-send all routes.  Note that the neigh‐
             bor is not obliged to re-send all routes, or any routes at all,
             even if it announced the route refresh capability.  _p_e_e_r may be
             the neighbor's address, description or the word ggrroouupp followed by
             a group description.

     nneeiigghhbboorr _p_e_e_r uupp
             Bring the BGP session to the specified neighbor up.  _p_e_e_r may be
             the neighbor's address, description or the word ggrroouupp followed by
             a group description.

     nneettwwoorrkk aadddd _p_r_e_f_i_x [_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t _._._.]
             Add the specified prefix to the list of announced networks.  It
             is possible to set various path attributes with additional argu‐
             ments.  Adding a prefix will replace an existing equal prefix,
             including prefixes loaded from the configuration.

     nneettwwoorrkk bbuullkk aadddd [_a_r_g_u_m_e_n_t _._._.]
             Bulk add specified prefixes to the list of announced networks.
             Prefixes should be sent via stdin.  It is possible to set various
             path attributes with additional arguments.

     nneettwwoorrkk bbuullkk ddeelleettee
             Bulk remove the specified prefixes from the list of announced
             networks.  Prefixes should be sent via stdin.

     nneettwwoorrkk ddeelleettee _p_r_e_f_i_x
             Remove the specified prefix from the list of announced networks.

     nneettwwoorrkk fflluusshh
             Remove all dynamically added (i.e. with bbggppccttll nneettwwoorrkk aadddd) pre‐
             fixes from the list of announced networks.

     nneettwwoorrkk mmrrtt ffiillee _f_i_l_e _f_i_l_t_e_r
             Import networks from an MRT table dump for debugging purposes.
             _f_i_l_t_e_r can be specified similarly to the _s_h_o_w _m_r_t command.  Only
             networks matching the filter will be imported.

     nneettwwoorrkk sshhooww _f_a_m_i_l_y
             Show all announced networks.  _f_a_m_i_l_y, if given, limits the output
             to the given address family.  The supported families are _i_n_e_t and
             _i_n_e_t_6.

     rreellooaadd [reason]
             Reload the configuration file.  Changes to the following neighbor
             options in bgpd.conf(5) only take effect when the session is re‐
             set: iippsseecc and ttccpp mmdd55ssiigg.

     sshhooww ffiibb _f_i_l_t_e_r
             Show routes from bgpd(8)'s view of the Forwarding Information
             Base.  _f_i_l_t_e_r can be an IP address, in which case the route to
             this address is shown, or a flag:

             bbggpp           Show only routes originating from bgpd(8) itself.
             ccoonnnneecctteedd     Show only connected routes.
             iinneett          Show only IPv4 routes.
             iinneett66         Show only IPv6 routes.
             nneexxtthhoopp       Show only routes required to reach a BGP nexthop.
             ssttaattiicc        Show only static routes.
             ttaabbllee _n_u_m_b_e_r  Show the routing table with ID _n_u_m_b_e_r instead of
                           the default routing table with ID 0.

     sshhooww iinntteerrffaacceess
             Show the interface states.

     sshhooww mmeettrriiccss
             Dump various BGP statistics in OpenMetrics format.

     sshhooww mmrrtt [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _f_i_l_t_e_r
             Show routes from an MRT table dump file.  _f_i_l_t_e_r can be an IP ad‐
             dress, a CIDR prefix, an AS filter, a combination or nothing:

             _a_d_d_r_e_s_s                 Show best matching route for address.
             _a_d_d_r_e_s_s/_l_e_n             Show RIB entry for this CIDR prefix.
             _a_d_d_r_e_s_s/_l_e_n aallll         Show all entries in the specified range.
             _a_d_d_r_e_s_s/_l_e_n oorr--sshhoorrtteerr  Show all entries covering and including
                                     the specified prefix.
             aass _a_s                   Show all entries with _a_s anywhere in the
                                     AS path.
             eemmppttyy--aass                Show all entries that are internal routes
                                     with no AS's in the AS path.
             nneeiigghhbboorr _i_p             Show only entries from the specified
                                     peer.
             ppeeeerr--aass _a_s              Show all entries with _a_s as leftmost AS.
             ssoouurrccee--aass _a_s            Show all entries with _a_s as rightmost AS.
             ttrraannssiitt--aass _a_s           Show all entries with _a_s anywhere but
                                     rightmost.

             Additionally, the following _o_p_t_i_o_n_s are defined:

             ddeettaaiill     Show more detailed output for matching routes.
             _f_a_m_i_l_y     Limit the output to the given address family.
             ffiillee _n_a_m_e  Read the MRT dump from file _n_a_m_e instead of using
                        stdin.
             ppeeeerrss      Print the neighbor table of MRT TABLE_DUMP_V2 dumps.
                        Using this on other table dumps will only show the
                        neighbor of the first entry.

             Multiple options and filters can be used at the same time.

     sshhooww nneeiigghhbboorr _p_e_e_r _m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r
             Show detailed information about the neighbor identified by _p_e_e_r,
             according to the given _m_o_d_i_f_i_e_r:

             mmeessssaaggeess  Show statistics about sent and received BGP messages.
             tteerrssee     Show statistics in an easily parseable terse format.
                       The printed numbers are the sent and received open,
                       sent and received notifications, sent and received up‐
                       dates, sent and received keepalives, and sent and re‐
                       ceived route refresh messages plus the current and max‐
                       imum prefix count, the number of sent and received up‐
                       dates, sent and received withdraws, the neighbor's ad‐
                       dress (or subnet, for a template), AS number, and fi‐
                       nally description.
             ttiimmeerrss    Show the BGP timers.
             _p_e_e_r may be the neighbor's address, description or the word ggrroouupp
             followed by a group description.

     sshhooww nneexxtthhoopp
             Show the list of BGP nexthops and the result of their validity
             check.

     sshhooww rriibb [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s] _f_i_l_t_e_r
             Show routes from the bgpd(8) Routing Information Base.  _f_i_l_t_e_r
             can be an IP address, a CIDR prefix, an AS filter or nothing:

             _a_d_d_r_e_s_s                 Show best matching route for address.
             _a_d_d_r_e_s_s/_l_e_n             Show RIB entry for this CIDR prefix.
             _a_d_d_r_e_s_s/_l_e_n aallll         Show all entries in the specified range.
             _a_d_d_r_e_s_s/_l_e_n oorr--sshhoorrtteerr  Show all entries covering and including
                                     the specified prefix.
             aass _a_s                   Show all entries with _a_s anywhere in the
                                     AS path.
             aavvss (vvaalliidd | uunnkknnoowwnn | iinnvvaalliidd)
                                     Show all entries with matching ASAP Vali‐
                                     dation State (AVS).
             ccoommmmuunniittyy _c_o_m_m_u_n_i_t_y     Show all entries with community
                                     _c_o_m_m_u_n_i_t_y.
             eemmppttyy--aass                Show all entries that are internal routes
                                     with no AS's in the AS path.
             llaarrggee--ccoommmmuunniittyy _l_a_r_g_e_-_c_o_m_m_u_n_i_t_y
                                     Show all entries with large-community
                                     _l_a_r_g_e_-_c_o_m_m_u_n_i_t_y.
             mmeemmoorryy                  Show RIB memory statistics.
             nneeiigghhbboorr _p_e_e_r           Show only entries from the specified
                                     peer.
             nneeiigghhbboorr ggrroouupp _d_e_s_c_r_i_p_t_i_o_n
                                     Show only entries from the specified peer
                                     group.
             oovvss (vvaalliidd | nnoott--ffoouunndd | iinnvvaalliidd)
                                     Show all entries with matching Origin
                                     Validation State (OVS).
             ppaatthh--iidd _p_a_t_h_i_d          Show only entries which match the speci‐
                                     fied _p_a_t_h_i_d.  Must be used together with
                                     either nneeiigghhbboorr or oouutt.
             ppeeeerr--aass _a_s              Show all entries with _a_s as leftmost AS.
             ssoouurrccee--aass _a_s            Show all entries with _a_s as rightmost AS.
             ssuummmmaarryy                 This is the same as the sshhooww ssuummmmaarryy com‐
                                     mand.
             ttaabbllee _r_i_b               Show only entries from the specified RIB
                                     table.
             ttrraannssiitt--aass _a_s           Show all entries with _a_s anywhere but
                                     rightmost.

             Additionally, the following _o_p_t_i_o_n_s are defined:

             bbeesstt          Alias for sseelleecctteedd.
             ddeettaaiill        Show more detailed output for matching routes.
             ddiissqquuaalliiffiieedd  Show only routes which are not eligible.
             eerrrroorr         Show only prefixes which are marked invalid and
                           were treated as withdrawn.
             _f_a_m_i_l_y        Limit the output to the given address family.
             ffiilltteerreedd      Show only routes which were filtered out.  Requires
                           rrddee rriibb LLoocc--RRIIBB iinncclluuddee ffiilltteerreedd to be set in the
                           config.
             iinn            Show routes from the unfiltered Adj-RIB-In.  The
                           nneeiigghhbboorr needs to be specified.
             lleeaakkeedd        Show only routes where a route leak was detected.
             oouutt           Show the filtered routes sent to a neighbor.  The
                           nneeiigghhbboorr needs to be specified.
             sseelleecctteedd      Show only selected routes.
             ssssvv           Show each RIB entry as a single line, with fields
                           separated by semicolons.  Only works if ddeettaaiill is
                           specified.

             Options are silently ignored when used together with _s_u_m_m_a_r_y or
             _m_e_m_o_r_y.  Multiple options can be used at the same time and the
             _n_e_i_g_h_b_o_r filter can be combined with other filters.

     sshhooww rrttrr
             Show a list of all _R_T_R sessions, including information about the
             session state.

     sshhooww sseettss
             Show a list summarizing all _r_o_a_-_s_e_t, _a_s_-_s_e_t, _p_r_e_f_i_x_-_s_e_t, and
             _o_r_i_g_i_n_-_s_e_t tables.

     sshhooww ssuummmmaarryy
             Show a list of all neighbors, including information about the
             session state and message counters:

             Neighbor        Description of the neighbor.
             AS              Autonomous system number.
             MsgRcvd         Number of messages received from the neighbor.
             MsgSent         Number of messages sent to the neighbor.
             OutQ            Number of outgoing messages queued.
             Up/Down         Number of days and hours that the session has
                             been up.
             State/PrfRcvd   State of the session / Number of routes received.
                             The session is up if there is no information for
                             the State column (Established is not displayed).

     sshhooww ssuummmmaarryy tteerrssee
             Show a list of all neighbors, including information about the
             session state, in a terse format.

     sshhooww ttaabblleess
             Show a list of all currently loaded fib routing tables.

